# Cross-platform byte determinism.
#
# This repo hashes file bytes. sha256(file_bytes) is the identity of every shard,
# manifest and content hash in the system. The machine this was authored on has
# core.autocrlf=true, so without this file the vendored corpus would be checked
# out with CRLF here and LF on a Linux grader's machine -- and every hash in
# submission_artifacts/ would silently fail to reproduce.
#
# Rule: text files normalise to LF in the working tree; anything whose exact
# bytes are load-bearing is marked binary so git never touches it.

# ORDER MATTERS: in a .gitattributes file the LAST matching pattern wins. An
# earlier version of this file listed the byte-critical paths first and the
# per-extension rules last, so `*.json text eol=lf` silently overrode
# `corpus/** -text` for every manifest and every documents.jsonl -- verified with
# `git check-attr -a`, which reported `text: set` on exactly the files this file
# exists to protect. The byte-critical block must come last.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Source types: normalise line endings, they are read by humans and diffed.
*.py    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf

# Bytes ARE the identity here, so git must never touch them. Last, so these win
# over anything above. Check with:
#   git check-attr -a corpus/indic/documents.jsonl   ->  text: unset
corpus/**                   -text
submission_artifacts/**     -text
submission_artifacts_*/**   -text
*.bin                       -text
*.shard                     -text
